#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Select the tests a branch can affect: the core set plus every test that
imports, directly or transitively, a module the branch changed (#356).

Local runs are a fast feedback loop, not the gate. CI on the pull request
runs the full suite and the full matrix runs on `dev` after the merge, so a
local miss costs one red PR run -- which is why this selector is static and
lean rather than coverage-based. What it must not do is quietly skip a test
that imports what changed, and it falls back to the full suite when the
branch touches something every test depends on.

Selection
  core       tests marked ``@pytest.mark.core`` -- a small smoke set over
             storage, the KB service, auth/read scoping, the REST app
             factory, MCP dispatch and the CLI entry point (pinned, file by
             file, in tests/test_test_affected.py). Always runs.
  affected   test files that import a changed module, directly or through
             any chain of imports; built with ``ast`` over pyrite/,
             extensions/*/src/, scripts/ and the test trees, so no database
             and no imports are executed. Counted as imports: ``import`` and
             ``from`` statements anywhere in a file (function-level too),
             and dotted string literals naming a module -- ``mock.patch``
             targets and ``importlib.import_module`` arguments. A test that
             uses a non-autouse conftest fixture inherits what that fixture
             (and the fixtures and helpers it calls) imports. Changed test
             files are always selected. A changed non-Python file selects
             the modules and tests that name it (its basename, else its
             directory's name).
  full suite when the branch touches conftest.py, a pyproject.toml,
             pytest.ini / setup.cfg / tox.ini, .pre-commit-config.yaml,
             .github/workflows/, anything under a tests' fixtures/
             directory, or a test helper module imported by at least
             ``--helper-threshold`` test files.

A change under extensions/*/src/ also selects every test that mentions
``get_registry``, ``entry_points`` or ``PluginRegistry``: core tests reach
plugins through entry points, which no import names.

Experimental tests (#657) are deselected: ``--run`` passes ``-m "not slow and
not e2e and not experimental"``, the gating CI job's expression, so a push is
held to what a merge is. ``--experimental`` or ``PYRITE_PUSH_EXPERIMENTAL=1``
runs them too. A pass is stamped per scope: a core-only pass never vouches for
a run that includes experimental tests.

Known blind spots, by design (CI covers them): autouse fixtures are not
attributed to the tests they wrap. Importing ``pkg.x`` executes
``pkg/__init__.py``, and so everything that ``__init__`` imports; that
import-time reach is not followed (``from pkg import Name`` resolves to the
module defining ``Name``), so a module's import-time side effects select
only its own importers.

Usage
  scripts/test-affected --list                 selected paths / node ids
  scripts/test-affected --explain              why each one was selected
  scripts/test-affected --run [-n N]           run them (default -n 4)
  scripts/test-affected --run --full           run the full suite instead
                                               (or PYRITE_PUSH_FULL=1|true|yes)
  scripts/test-affected --run -- --lf          re-run only the last run's failures
                                               (anything after -- goes to pytest)
  --force             ignore pass stamps (or PYRITE_PUSH_FORCE=1|true|yes)
  --base REF          compare against REF's merge base (default PYRITE_BASE, upstream/dev, origin/dev, dev)
  --committed         ignore uncommitted and untracked changes
  --files A B ...     use these changed paths instead of asking git

Under the pre-push hook (PRE_COMMIT_FROM_REF / PRE_COMMIT_TO_REF set) the
changed paths are those of the pushed range, not the working tree.

Pass stamps: each tree is tested once
  A ``--run`` that exits 0 on a clean tree (no uncommitted change to a
  tracked file, no untracked ``.py`` file) records a stamp: the HEAD tree SHA,
  the interpreter's major.minor, and the selection it ran ("full" for the
  full suite). A later ``--run`` -- the pre-push hook's included -- whose
  selection the stamp covers skips, printing one line. The worker's own run
  and the hook's run of the same commit were the same tests on the same code;
  under load the repeat cost about 45 minutes (maintainer, 2026-09-25).

  The tree looked up is the one being tested: the pushed ref's tree under the
  hook, else HEAD's tree when the working tree is clean (a dirty tree never
  skips and is never stamped). Stamps live under the git COMMON dir, so every
  worktree shares them; a tree SHA names content, so that is safe. "full"
  covers any selection; a list covers another when it contains each test (a
  file covers its node ids). Two passes on one tree merge. Never stamped: a
  failure, a dry run, and a run whose pytest arguments can narrow it
  (``--lf``, ``-k``, ...; only output flags such as ``-v``, ``-x``,
  ``--tb=`` keep a run stampable; PYTEST_ADDOPTS or PYTEST_PLUGINS set
  means not stampable), and a run without the worktree's own ``.venv``. A
  stamp that does not parse, names another tree or interpreter, or is older
  than 14 days is ignored, and old ones are pruned when a new one is written.

  "Clean" also means nothing the tree does not hold can reach a test: no
  untracked ``.py`` file anywhere, and no untracked or gitignored file under
  pyrite/, tests/, extensions/, kb/ or scripts/ other than build noise
  (__pycache__, *.pyc, *.egg-info, .pytest_cache).

  What a stamp does NOT key on: the environment. It is the tree plus the
  Python major.minor -- not the installed packages, not environment
  variables, not services. A pass where the Postgres tests skipped
  (PYRITE_TEST_PG_URL unset) covers a later run where it is set; a pass
  before a ``uv pip install`` that left pyproject alone covers a run after
  it. When the environment is what changed, run with ``--force``
  (PYRITE_PUSH_FORCE=1 under the hook).
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import ast
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
from collections import deque
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
from base_ref import HINT, BaseRefError, resolve_base

DEFAULT_WORKERS = 4
DEFAULT_HELPER_THRESHOLD = 20

_FULL_SUITE_NAMES = {"conftest.py", "pyproject.toml", "pytest.ini", "setup.cfg", "tox.ini"}
_FULL_SUITE_PATHS = {".pre-commit-config.yaml"}
_FULL_SUITE_PREFIXES = (".github/workflows/",)
# Content trees: a change here is matched by basename only, never by its
# directory's name ("backlog", "docs" are words half the suite uses).
_CONTENT_TOPS = {"kb", "docs", "web"}
# Directory names too common to stand for a file in them: "tests" and
# "scripts" appear in a third of the suite.
_GENERIC_DIRS = {"tests", "scripts"}
_SKIP_DIRS = {"__pycache__", ".venv", "node_modules", ".git", "build", "dist"}
# A change inside an extension's package also selects the tests that load
# plugins through entry points, which no import names.
_EXTENSION_SRC = re.compile(r"^extensions/[^/]+/src/")
_LOADS_PLUGINS = re.compile(r"\b(get_registry|entry_points|PluginRegistry)\b")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Changed files


def _git(root: Path, *args: str) -> str:
    return subprocess.run(
        ["git", *args], cwd=root, check=True, capture_output=True, text=True
    ).stdout


def changed_files(root: Path, base: str | None = None, committed_only: bool = False) -> list[str]:
    """Paths changed since the merge base of ``base`` and HEAD.

    Renames are reported as both paths (a module that moved still has
    importers of its old name). Unless ``committed_only``, the working tree's
    uncommitted edits and untracked files count too -- during work, the test
    you want run is the one for the edit you have not committed yet.
    """
    merge_base = _git(root, "merge-base", resolve_base(root, base), "HEAD").strip()
    # -z: without it git quotes and octal-escapes non-ASCII paths, which
    # then match nothing.
    if committed_only:
        out = _git(root, "diff", "-z", "--name-only", "--no-renames", merge_base, "HEAD")
    else:
        out = _git(root, "diff", "-z", "--name-only", "--no-renames", merge_base)
        out += _git(root, "ls-files", "-z", "--others", "--exclude-standard")
    return sorted({path for path in out.split("\0") if path.strip()})


def pushed_files(root: Path, from_ref: str | None, to_ref: str, base: str) -> list[str]:
    """Paths changed by a push of ``from_ref..to_ref`` (pre-commit's
    PRE_COMMIT_FROM_REF / PRE_COMMIT_TO_REF). With no usable ``from_ref``
    (a new branch, all zeros), from the merge base of ``base`` and ``to_ref``."""
    if not from_ref or not from_ref.strip("0"):
        from_ref = _git(root, "merge-base", base, to_ref).strip()
    out = _git(root, "diff", "-z", "--name-only", "--no-renames", from_ref, to_ref)
    return sorted({path for path in out.split("\0") if path.strip()})


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The repository as an import graph


@dataclass
class _File:
    path: str  # repo-relative, posix
    names: list[str]  # module names this file answers to
    is_test: bool
    text: str = ""
    # imported dotted name -> how ("" for a plain import, "fixture x (conftest)")
    deps: dict[str, str] = field(default_factory=dict)


def _walk_py(directory: Path):
    if not directory.is_dir():
        return
    for path in sorted(directory.rglob("*.py")):
        if not _SKIP_DIRS.intersection(path.parts):
            yield path


def _module_name(rel: Path, base: Path) -> str:
    parts = list(rel.relative_to(base).with_suffix("").parts)
    if parts[-1] == "__init__":
        parts = parts[:-1]
    return ".".join(parts)


def _prefixes(dotted: str) -> list[str]:
    parts = dotted.split(".")
    return [".".join(parts[: i + 1]) for i in range(len(parts))]


def _source_roots(root: Path) -> list[tuple[Path, Path]]:
    """(directory to walk, directory module names are relative to)."""
    roots = [(root / "pyrite", root)]
    roots += [(src, src) for src in sorted((root / "extensions").glob("*/src"))]
    roots.append((root / "scripts", root / "scripts"))
    return roots


def _test_roots(root: Path) -> list[Path]:
    return [root / "tests", *sorted((root / "extensions").glob("*/tests"))]


def _absolute(node: ast.ImportFrom, package: str) -> str:
    if not node.level:
        return node.module or ""
    parts = package.split(".") if package else []
    if node.level > 1:
        parts = parts[: len(parts) - (node.level - 1)]
    base = ".".join(parts)
    return f"{base}.{node.module}" if node.module and base else (node.module or base)


def _imports(tree: ast.AST, package: str) -> list[tuple[str, list[str]]]:
    """(module, names imported from it) for every import anywhere in ``tree``;
    a plain ``import a.b`` is ``("a.b", [])``."""
    records = []
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if isinstance(node, ast.Import):
            records += [(alias.name, []) for alias in node.names]
        elif isinstance(node, ast.ImportFrom):
            mod = _absolute(node, package)
            if mod:
                records.append((mod, [a.name for a in node.names if a.name != "*"]))
    return records


def _string_names(tree: ast.AST, tops: set[str]) -> set[str]:
    """Dotted string literals that name one of our modules (patch targets)."""
    names = set()
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if isinstance(node, ast.Constant) and isinstance(node.value, str):
            value = node.value
            if "." in value and value.split(".", 1)[0] in tops and _DOTTED.match(value):
                names.add(value)
    return names


_DOTTED = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z_]\w*(\.[A-Za-z_]\w*)+$")


def _is_fixture(decorator: ast.expr) -> tuple[bool, bool]:
    """(is a pytest fixture, is autouse)."""
    target = decorator.func if isinstance(decorator, ast.Call) else decorator
    name = target.attr if isinstance(target, ast.Attribute) else getattr(target, "id", "")
    if name != "fixture":
        return False, False
    autouse = isinstance(decorator, ast.Call) and any(
        kw.arg == "autouse" and isinstance(kw.value, ast.Constant) and kw.value.value
        for kw in decorator.keywords
    )
    return True, autouse


@dataclass
class _Fixture:
    name: str
    conftest: str
    autouse: bool
    imports: set[str]
    params: list[str]


def _conftest_fixtures(tree: ast.Module, package: str, rel: str, resolve) -> dict[str, _Fixture]:
    """Each fixture's own imports: in its body, module-level names it uses,
    and module-level helper functions it calls (transitively)."""
    bindings: dict[str, set[str]] = {}
    functions: dict[str, ast.FunctionDef] = {}
    for node in tree.body:
        if isinstance(node, ast.Import):
            for alias in node.names:
                bound = alias.asname or alias.name.split(".")[0]
                bindings.setdefault(bound, set()).update(resolve(alias.name, []))
        elif isinstance(node, ast.ImportFrom):
            mod = _absolute(node, package)
            for alias in node.names:
                if mod and alias.name != "*":
                    bindings.setdefault(alias.asname or alias.name, set()).update(
                        resolve(mod, [alias.name])
                    )
        elif isinstance(node, ast.FunctionDef | ast.AsyncFunctionDef):
            functions[node.name] = node

    def deps_of(fn: ast.AST, seen: set[str]) -> set[str]:
        found = {d for mod, names in _imports(fn, package) for d in resolve(mod, names)}
        for node in ast.walk(fn):
            if isinstance(node, ast.Name):
                if node.id in bindings:
                    found |= bindings[node.id]
                elif node.id in functions and node.id not in seen:
                    seen.add(node.id)
                    found |= deps_of(functions[node.id], seen)
        return found

    fixtures = {}
    for name, fn in functions.items():
        for decorator in fn.decorator_list:
            is_fixture, autouse = _is_fixture(decorator)
            if is_fixture:
                params = [a.arg for a in fn.args.args + fn.args.kwonlyargs]
                fixtures[name] = _Fixture(name, rel, autouse, deps_of(fn, {name}), params)
                break
    return fixtures


def _used_fixture_names(tree: ast.AST) -> set[str]:
    used = set()
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if isinstance(node, ast.FunctionDef | ast.AsyncFunctionDef):
            used.update(a.arg for a in node.args.args + node.args.kwonlyargs)
        elif isinstance(node, ast.Call):
            func = node.func
            if isinstance(func, ast.Attribute) and func.attr == "usefixtures":
                used.update(
                    a.value
                    for a in node.args
                    if isinstance(a, ast.Constant) and isinstance(a.value, str)
                )
    return used


def _core_ids(rel: str, tree: ast.Module) -> list[str]:
    def is_core(expr: ast.expr) -> bool:
        target = expr.func if isinstance(expr, ast.Call) else expr
        # pytest.mark.core, or mark.core after `from pytest import mark`
        if not (isinstance(target, ast.Attribute) and target.attr == "core"):
            return False
        owner = target.value
        return (isinstance(owner, ast.Attribute) and owner.attr == "mark") or (
            isinstance(owner, ast.Name) and owner.id == "mark"
        )

    for node in tree.body:
        if isinstance(node, ast.Assign):
            targets, value = node.targets, node.value
        elif isinstance(node, ast.AnnAssign) and node.value is not None:
            targets, value = [node.target], node.value
        else:
            continue
        if any(isinstance(t, ast.Name) and t.id == "pytestmark" for t in targets):
            values = value.elts if isinstance(value, ast.List | ast.Tuple) else [value]
            if any(is_core(v) for v in values):
                return [rel]
    ids = []
    for node in tree.body:
        if isinstance(node, ast.ClassDef | ast.FunctionDef | ast.AsyncFunctionDef):
            if any(is_core(d) for d in node.decorator_list):
                ids.append(f"{rel}::{node.name}")
            elif isinstance(node, ast.ClassDef):
                for item in node.body:
                    if isinstance(item, ast.FunctionDef | ast.AsyncFunctionDef) and any(
                        is_core(d) for d in item.decorator_list
                    ):
                        ids.append(f"{rel}::{node.name}::{item.name}")
    return ids


class Graph:
    def __init__(self, root: Path):
        self.root = root
        self.files: dict[str, _File] = {}
        self.core: list[str] = []
        self._trees: dict[str, ast.Module] = {}
        self._packages: dict[str, str] = {}
        test_roots = _test_roots(root)

        for walk_dir, base in _source_roots(root):
            for path in _walk_py(walk_dir):
                self._add(path, [_module_name(path, base)], is_test=False)
        for troot in test_roots:
            # tests/ is a package (tests.x); extensions/*/tests are rootdir
            # modules imported by their bare name.
            base = troot.parent if (troot / "__init__.py").exists() else troot
            for path in _walk_py(troot):
                is_test = path.name.startswith("test_")
                self._add(path, [_module_name(path, base)], is_test=is_test)

        self.by_name: dict[str, list[str]] = {}
        for f in self.files.values():
            for name in f.names:
                self.by_name.setdefault(name, []).append(f.path)
        self._reexports = {
            f.names[0]: self._reexport_map(rel)
            for rel, f in self.files.items()
            if rel.endswith("__init__.py")
        }

        tops = {n.split(".")[0] for f in self.files.values() for n in f.names if n}
        for rel, tree in self._trees.items():
            f = self.files[rel]
            package = self._packages[rel]
            for mod, names in _imports(tree, package):
                for dep in self.resolve(mod, names):
                    f.deps.setdefault(dep, "")
            for dotted in _string_names(tree, tops):
                for dep in self.resolve(dotted, []):
                    f.deps.setdefault(dep, "")
            if f.is_test:
                self.core += _core_ids(rel, tree)

        self._attribute_fixtures(test_roots)

        self.importers: dict[str, list[tuple[str, str]]] = {}
        for f in self.files.values():
            for name, how in f.deps.items():
                self.importers.setdefault(name, []).append((f.path, how))

    def _reexport_map(self, rel: str) -> dict[str, str]:
        """Names a package's __init__ binds by importing them: name -> module."""
        found = {}
        for node in self._trees[rel].body:
            if isinstance(node, ast.ImportFrom):
                mod = _absolute(node, self._packages[rel])
                for alias in node.names:
                    if mod and alias.name != "*":
                        found[alias.asname or alias.name] = f"{mod}.{alias.name}"
        return found

    def _module_of(self, dotted: str) -> str:
        """The longest prefix of ``dotted`` that is one of our modules, else
        ``dotted`` itself (a deleted module is still matched by name)."""
        for prefix in reversed(_prefixes(dotted)):
            if prefix in self.by_name:
                return prefix
        return dotted

    def resolve(self, mod: str, names: list[str]) -> set[str]:
        """The modules an import depends on.

        ``from pkg import Name`` depends on ``pkg.Name`` when that is a
        module, else on the module ``pkg/__init__.py`` took ``Name`` from
        (followed through chains of re-exports), else on ``pkg``. Parent
        packages are not added: that ``import pkg.x`` also executes
        ``pkg/__init__.py`` is handled from the other side, when an
        ``__init__`` itself changes (see ``select``).
        """
        # A module we cannot find is kept by name as well: it may be the
        # deleted module the branch is about.
        deps = {mod} if mod not in self.by_name else set()
        if not names:
            return deps | {self._module_of(mod)}
        for name in names:
            target = f"{mod}.{name}"
            for _ in range(8):
                if target in self.by_name:
                    break
                pkg, _, attr = target.rpartition(".")
                source = self._reexports.get(pkg, {}).get(attr)
                if source is None:
                    break
                target = source
            deps.add(target if target in self.by_name else self._module_of(target))
        return deps

    def _add(self, path: Path, names: list[str], is_test: bool) -> None:
        rel = path.relative_to(self.root).as_posix()
        text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
        try:
            tree = ast.parse(text, filename=rel)
        except SyntaxError:
            tree = ast.Module(body=[], type_ignores=[])
        name = names[0]
        package = name if path.name == "__init__.py" else name.rpartition(".")[0]
        self.files[rel] = _File(rel, names, is_test, text)
        self._trees[rel] = tree
        self._packages[rel] = package

    def _attribute_fixtures(self, test_roots: list[Path]) -> None:
        conftests: dict[str, dict[str, _Fixture]] = {}
        for rel, tree in self._trees.items():
            if rel.endswith("conftest.py"):
                conftests[rel] = _conftest_fixtures(tree, self._packages[rel], rel, self.resolve)
        root_conftest = self.root / "conftest.py"
        if root_conftest.exists():
            tree = ast.parse(root_conftest.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
            conftests["conftest.py"] = _conftest_fixtures(tree, "", "conftest.py", self.resolve)

        for rel, f in self.files.items():
            if not f.is_test:
                continue
            # Nearest conftest first, so a closer fixture shadows a farther one.
            scope = sorted(
                (
                    c
                    for c in conftests
                    if rel.startswith(c.rpartition("/")[0] + "/") or c == "conftest.py"
                ),
                key=lambda c: -c.count("/") if c != "conftest.py" else 1,
            )

            def lookup(name: str, scope: list[str] = scope) -> _Fixture | None:
                for c in scope:
                    if name in conftests[c]:
                        return conftests[c][name]
                return None

            used = _used_fixture_names(self._trees[rel])
            seen: set[str] = set()
            stack = list(used)
            while stack:
                name = stack.pop()
                if name in seen:
                    continue
                seen.add(name)
                fixture = lookup(name)
                # An autouse fixture is attributed only to a test that names
                # it; it is never "used" by the tests it silently wraps.
                if fixture is None:
                    continue
                for dep in fixture.imports:
                    f.deps.setdefault(dep, f"fixture {fixture.name} ({fixture.conftest})")
                stack.extend(fixture.params)

    def module_names_for(self, rel: str) -> list[str]:
        """Module names a (possibly deleted) path answers to."""
        if rel in self.files:
            return self.files[rel].names
        path = self.root / rel
        if path.suffix != ".py":
            return []
        for walk_dir, base in _source_roots(self.root):
            if path.is_relative_to(walk_dir):
                return [_module_name(path, base)]
        for troot in _test_roots(self.root):
            if path.is_relative_to(troot):
                base = troot.parent if (troot / "__init__.py").exists() else troot
                return [_module_name(path, base)]
        return []

    def in_test_root(self, rel: str) -> bool:
        path = self.root / rel
        return any(path.is_relative_to(t) for t in _test_roots(self.root))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Selection


@dataclass
class Selection:
    files: dict[str, str]  # test path -> why
    core: list[str]
    full: str | None = None  # the reason the full suite runs, if it does


def _full_suite_reason(graph: Graph, rel: str) -> str | None:
    name = rel.rpartition("/")[2]
    if name in _FULL_SUITE_NAMES:
        return f"{rel} ({name} configures every test)"
    if rel in _FULL_SUITE_PATHS or rel.startswith(_FULL_SUITE_PREFIXES):
        return f"{rel} (hook or CI configuration)"
    if graph.in_test_root(rel) and "fixtures" in Path(rel).parts:
        return f"{rel} (shared test fixture data)"
    return None


def _referencing_files(graph: Graph, rel: str) -> tuple[list[str], str]:
    """Files that name a non-module path: its basename, else its directory."""
    path = Path(rel)
    source_root = next(
        (walk for walk, _ in _source_roots(graph.root) if (graph.root / rel).is_relative_to(walk)),
        None,
    )

    def candidates(f: _File) -> bool:
        if f.path == rel:
            return False
        if f.is_test:
            return True
        return source_root is not None and (graph.root / f.path).is_relative_to(source_root)

    pool = [f for f in graph.files.values() if candidates(f)]
    needles = [path.name]
    if path.suffix == ".py":
        needles.append(path.stem)
    if (
        len(path.parts) > 1
        and path.parts[0] not in _CONTENT_TOPS
        and path.parent.name not in _GENERIC_DIRS
    ):
        needles.append(path.parent.name)
    for needle in needles:
        word = re.compile(rf"(?<![\w.-]){re.escape(needle)}(?![\w-])")
        hits = [f.path for f in pool if needle in f.text and word.search(f.text)]
        if hits:
            return hits, needle
    return [], ""


def select(
    root: Path,
    changed: list[str],
    helper_threshold: int = DEFAULT_HELPER_THRESHOLD,
    graph: Graph | None = None,
) -> Selection:
    root = Path(root).resolve()
    graph = graph or Graph(root)
    selection = Selection(files={}, core=list(graph.core))

    for rel in changed:
        reason = _full_suite_reason(graph, rel)
        if reason:
            selection.full = reason
            return selection

    # reached[name] = (the name it was reached from, or None for a seed; seed label)
    reached: dict[str, tuple[str | None, str]] = {}
    queue: deque[str] = deque()

    def seed(name: str, label: str) -> None:
        if name not in reached:
            reached[name] = (None, label)
            queue.append(name)

    def chain(name: str) -> str:
        parts = []
        while True:
            prev, label = reached[name]
            parts.append(name)
            if prev is None:
                return " -> ".join(parts) + f" ({label})"
            name = prev

    helpers: list[str] = []
    packages: set[str] = set()  # changed __init__ modules
    plugin_change = next((r for r in changed if _EXTENSION_SRC.match(r)), None)
    if plugin_change:
        # Core tests reach extensions through entry points, not imports.
        for f in graph.files.values():
            if f.is_test and _LOADS_PLUGINS.search(f.text):
                selection.files.setdefault(f.path, f"loads plugins ({plugin_change})")
    for rel in changed:
        f = graph.files.get(rel)
        if f is not None and f.is_test:
            selection.files[rel] = "changed test file"
        names = graph.module_names_for(rel)
        if names:
            for name in names:
                seed(name, rel)
                if rel.endswith("__init__.py"):
                    packages.add(name)
            if graph.in_test_root(rel) and not Path(rel).name.startswith("test_"):
                helpers.append(rel)
        if not names or not rel.startswith(("pyrite/", "extensions/")):
            hits, needle = _referencing_files(graph, rel)
            for hit in hits:
                hf = graph.files[hit]
                if hf.is_test:
                    selection.files.setdefault(hit, f"names {needle!r} ({rel})")
                for name in hf.names:
                    seed(name, f"names {needle!r} ({rel})")

    def importers_of(name: str) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
        found = list(graph.importers.get(name, []))
        if name in packages:
            # Importing any submodule executes the package's __init__.
            prefix = name + "."
            for dep, users in graph.importers.items():
                if dep.startswith(prefix):
                    found += users
        return found

    while queue:
        name = queue.popleft()
        for importer, how in importers_of(name):
            f = graph.files[importer]
            if f.is_test and importer not in selection.files:
                via = f"{how}: " if how else "imports "
                selection.files[importer] = via + chain(name)
            for own in f.names:
                if own not in reached:
                    reached[own] = (name, reached[name][1])
                    queue.append(own)

    for rel in helpers:
        users = _tests_reaching(graph, graph.module_names_for(rel))
        if len(users) >= helper_threshold:
            selection.full = (
                f"{rel} (test helper imported by {len(users)} test files, "
                f"threshold {helper_threshold})"
            )
            selection.files = {}
            return selection

    return selection


def _tests_reaching(graph: Graph, names: list[str]) -> set[str]:
    """Test files that import any of ``names``, directly or transitively."""
    seen = set(names)
    queue = deque(names)
    tests = set()
    while queue:
        for importer, _ in graph.importers.get(queue.popleft(), []):
            f = graph.files[importer]
            if f.is_test:
                tests.add(importer)
            for own in f.names:
                if own not in seen:
                    seen.add(own)
                    queue.append(own)
    return tests


def _workers(value: str) -> str:
    if value == "auto" or (value.isdigit() and int(value) > 0):
        return value
    raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError(f"{value!r}: a positive number or 'auto'")


def pytest_args(selection: Selection, workers: int | str = DEFAULT_WORKERS) -> list[str]:
    args = ["-n", str(workers)]
    if selection.full is not None:
        return [*args, "tests/", "extensions/"]
    files = sorted(selection.files)
    extra = [c for c in selection.core if c.split("::")[0] not in selection.files]
    return [*args, *files, *extra]


def _test_ids(path: Path, rel: str) -> list[str]:
    """Node ids (without parameters) of the test functions in one file."""
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, SyntaxError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        return []
    ids: list[str] = []

    def walk(node: ast.AST, prefix: list[str]) -> None:
        for child in ast.iter_child_nodes(node):
            if isinstance(child, ast.ClassDef):
                walk(child, [*prefix, child.name])
            elif isinstance(child, ast.FunctionDef | ast.AsyncFunctionDef):
                if child.name.startswith("test"):
                    ids.append("::".join([rel, *prefix, child.name]))

    walk(tree, [])
    return ids


def experimental_left_out(root: Path, items: list[str]) -> tuple[int, int] | None:
    """(tests, files) among the selection that the mapping marks experimental,
    which the default --run deselects (#657). None without a mapping."""
    mapping = root / "tests" / "experimental_surface.py"
    if not mapping.exists():
        return None
    try:
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("_experimental_surface", mapping)
        module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
        # Registered first: its dataclasses look their module up by name.
        sys.modules["_experimental_surface"] = module
        spec.loader.exec_module(module)
        is_experimental = module.is_experimental
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - a note, never a reason to block a run
        return None
    tests, files = 0, set()
    for item in items:
        target = item.split("::", 1)[0]
        path = root / target
        paths = sorted(path.rglob("test_*.py")) if path.is_dir() else [path]
        for test_file in paths:
            rel = test_file.relative_to(root).as_posix()
            for nodeid in _test_ids(test_file, rel):
                if "::" in item and not (nodeid == item or nodeid.startswith(item + "::")):
                    continue
                if is_experimental(nodeid):
                    tests += 1
                    files.add(rel)
    return tests, len(files)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pass stamps: each tree is tested once

STAMP_DIR_NAME = "pyrite-test-stamps"
STAMP_VERSION = 1
STAMP_MAX_AGE = 14 * 86400  # seconds
# pytest arguments that change how a run reports, never which tests run: a
# pass with only these ran the whole selection. Anything else (--lf, -k,
# --deselect, a path) may have run part of it, so that pass is not stamped.
_OUTPUT_ONLY_ARGS = {
    "-v", "-vv", "-vvv", "-q", "-qq", "-x", "--exitfirst", "-s", "-l",
    "--showlocals", "--no-header", "--no-summary",
}  # fmt: skip
_OUTPUT_ONLY_PREFIXES = ("--tb=", "--durations=", "--color=", "-r", "--capture=")


# The gating CI job's marker expression (#657). It replaces pyproject's
# addopts -m, so it restates "not slow and not e2e".
CORE_ONLY = "not slow and not e2e and not experimental"


def _truthy(name: str) -> bool:
    return os.environ.get(name, "").strip().lower() in {"1", "true", "yes"}


def python_version(python: str) -> str | None:
    """major.minor of the interpreter that runs pytest, or None if unknown."""
    if python == sys.executable:
        return f"{sys.version_info[0]}.{sys.version_info[1]}"
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(
            [python, "-c", "import sys; print('%d.%d' % sys.version_info[:2])"],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            timeout=60,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    version = out.stdout.strip()
    return version if out.returncode == 0 and re.fullmatch(r"\d+\.\d+", version) else None


def stamp_dir(root: Path) -> Path | None:
    """Under the git common dir: every worktree of the repository shares it."""
    try:
        common = Path(_git(root, "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir").strip())
    except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, OSError):
        return None
    return (common if common.is_absolute() else root / common) / STAMP_DIR_NAME


def tree_of(root: Path, rev: str) -> str | None:
    try:
        return _git(root, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"{rev}^{{tree}}").strip() or None
    except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, OSError):
        return None


# Where a file the tree does not hold can change what a test sees: code, test
# data, KB content, scripts. An untracked or gitignored file here (a
# tests/data.json a test reads, a fixture .db) passes the run, then is deleted,
# and the stamp would vouch for a tree that fails.
_CODE_ROOTS = ("pyrite", "tests", "extensions", "kb", "scripts")
# Generated by running or installing, never read as input by a test.
_BUILD_NOISE_DIRS = {"__pycache__", ".pytest_cache", ".mypy_cache", ".ruff_cache"}
_BUILD_NOISE_SUFFIXES = (".pyc", ".pyo")


def _is_build_noise(path: str) -> bool:
    parts = path.rstrip("/").split("/")
    return (
        any(p in _BUILD_NOISE_DIRS or p.endswith(".egg-info") for p in parts)
        or path.endswith(_BUILD_NOISE_SUFFIXES)
        or parts[-1] == ".DS_Store"
    )


def is_clean(root: Path) -> bool:
    """HEAD's tree is exactly what the tests could see: no uncommitted change
    to a tracked file, no untracked ``.py`` file anywhere (a root conftest or
    module changes what runs), and no untracked or gitignored file under the
    code roots other than build noise. Unknown counts as dirty."""
    try:
        if _git(root, "status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=no").strip():
            return False
        untracked = _git(root, "ls-files", "-z", "--others", "--exclude-standard")
        ignored = _git(
            root, "ls-files", "-z", "--others", "--ignored", "--exclude-standard",
            "--", *_CODE_ROOTS,
        )  # fmt: skip
    except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, OSError):
        return False
    for path in untracked.split("\0"):
        if path.endswith(".py") or (
            path.startswith(_CODE_ROOTS_PREFIXES) and not _is_build_noise(path)
        ):
            return False
    return not any(p and not _is_build_noise(p) for p in ignored.split("\0"))


_CODE_ROOTS_PREFIXES = tuple(f"{r}/" for r in _CODE_ROOTS)


# Environment variables pytest reads as extra arguments or plugins: either
# can narrow a run with nothing on the command line.
_PYTEST_ENV = ("PYTEST_ADDOPTS", "PYTEST_PLUGINS")


def stampable_args(extra: list[str]) -> bool:
    if any(os.environ.get(name, "").strip() for name in _PYTEST_ENV):
        return False
    return all(a in _OUTPUT_ONLY_ARGS or a.startswith(_OUTPUT_ONLY_PREFIXES) for a in extra)


def covers(stamped: str | list[str], needed: str | list[str]) -> bool:
    if stamped == "full":
        return True
    if needed == "full":
        return False
    have = set(stamped)
    return all(item in have or item.split("::")[0] in have for item in needed)


def _stamp_path(directory: Path, tree: str, python: str) -> Path:
    return directory / f"{tree}-py{python}.json"


def read_stamp(path: Path, tree: str, python: str, now: float | None = None) -> dict | None:
    """The stamp at ``path`` if it is well formed, for this tree and
    interpreter, and recent; else None. Never trust what does not parse."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    try:
        data = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    if not isinstance(data, dict) or data.get("version") != STAMP_VERSION:
        return None
    if data.get("tree") != tree or data.get("python") != python:
        return None
    sel = data.get("selection")
    if not (sel == "full" or (isinstance(sel, list) and all(isinstance(s, str) for s in sel))):
        return None
    when = data.get("when")
    if isinstance(when, bool) or not isinstance(when, int | float):
        return None
    if not now - STAMP_MAX_AGE <= when <= now + 300:
        return None
    return data


def write_stamp(directory: Path, tree: str, python: str, selection: str | list[str]) -> None:
    """Record a pass, merged with any valid stamp for the same tree; prune
    stamps older than STAMP_MAX_AGE. Best effort: a stamp that cannot be
    written costs one re-run, never a failure."""
    now = time.time()
    path = _stamp_path(directory, tree, python)
    previous = read_stamp(path, tree, python, now)
    if previous is not None:
        old = previous["selection"]
        if "full" in (old, selection):
            selection = "full"
        else:
            selection = sorted(set(old) | set(selection))
    elif selection != "full":
        selection = sorted(set(selection))
    data = {
        "version": STAMP_VERSION,
        "tree": tree,
        "python": python,
        "selection": selection,
        "when": now,
    }
    try:
        directory.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=directory, prefix=".stamp-", suffix=".tmp")
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            json.dump(data, fh)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
        for old_path in directory.iterdir():
            if old_path != path and now - old_path.stat().st_mtime > STAMP_MAX_AGE:
                old_path.unlink()
    except OSError:
        pass


def find_stamp(directory: Path, tree: str, python: str, needed: str | list[str]) -> dict | None:
    stamp = read_stamp(_stamp_path(directory, tree, python), tree, python)
    return stamp if stamp is not None and covers(stamp["selection"], needed) else None


def _skip_line(tree: str, stamp: dict) -> str:
    when = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M", time.localtime(stamp["when"]))
    sel = stamp["selection"]
    count = "full suite" if sel == "full" else f"{len(sel)} tests selected"
    return f"test-affected: already passed on tree {tree[:10]} ({when}, {count}); skipping"


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLI


def _python(root: Path) -> str:
    """The worktree's own interpreter: run from a shell, the shebang's
    ``python3`` may have no pytest, or import another checkout's code."""
    venv = root / ".venv" / "bin" / "python"
    return str(venv) if os.access(venv, os.X_OK) else sys.executable


def _default_base(root: Path, base: str | None) -> str:
    return resolve_base(root, base)


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Run the core tests plus the tests the branch can affect.",
    )
    mode = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    mode.add_argument("--list", action="store_true", help="print selected paths (default)")
    mode.add_argument("--explain", action="store_true", help="print why each test was selected")
    mode.add_argument("--run", action="store_true", help="run the selection with pytest")
    parser.add_argument(
        "-n", "--workers", type=_workers, default=str(DEFAULT_WORKERS), help="N or auto"
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--base", help="explicit integration ref (else PYRITE_BASE, upstream/dev, origin/dev, dev)"
    )
    parser.add_argument("--committed", action="store_true")
    parser.add_argument("--files", nargs="*", help="changed paths (skip git)")
    parser.add_argument("--full", action="store_true", help="select the full suite")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--experimental",
        action="store_true",
        help="with --run: include experimental tests (PYRITE_PUSH_EXPERIMENTAL=1)",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--helper-threshold", type=int, default=DEFAULT_HELPER_THRESHOLD)
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true", help="with --run: print the command only")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--force", action="store_true", help="with --run: ignore pass stamps (PYRITE_PUSH_FORCE=1)"
    )
    parser.add_argument("--root", default=None, help=argparse.SUPPRESS)
    parser.add_argument("pytest_extra", nargs=argparse.REMAINDER, help="after --: passed to pytest")
    opts = parser.parse_args(argv)

    root = Path(opts.root or Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent).resolve()
    env_full = _truthy("PYRITE_PUSH_FULL")
    to_ref = os.environ.get("PRE_COMMIT_TO_REF")
    if opts.full or env_full:
        selection = Selection(files={}, core=[], full="--full" if opts.full else "PYRITE_PUSH_FULL")
    else:
        try:
            if opts.files is not None:
                changed = opts.files
            elif to_ref:
                # Under the pre-push hook: what is being pushed, not the tree.
                base = _default_base(root, opts.base)
                changed = pushed_files(root, os.environ.get("PRE_COMMIT_FROM_REF"), to_ref, base)
            else:
                changed = changed_files(root, _default_base(root, opts.base), opts.committed)
        except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, BaseRefError) as exc:
            # Fail closed: selecting nothing would pass a push untested.
            detail = (getattr(exc, "stderr", None) or str(exc)).strip().splitlines()
            print(
                f"test-affected: could not diff against {opts.base!r}"
                + (f" ({detail[-1]})" if detail else "")
                + f" -- {HINT}, or push with PYRITE_PUSH_FULL=1",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return 2
        selection = select(root, changed, opts.helper_threshold)

    if opts.explain:
        if selection.full is not None:
            print(f"full suite: {selection.full}")
            return 0
        print(f"core ({len(selection.core)}):")
        for node in selection.core:
            print(f"  {node}")
        print(f"affected ({len(selection.files)}):")
        for path, why in sorted(selection.files.items()):
            print(f"  {path}  <- {why}")
        return 0

    args = pytest_args(selection, opts.workers)
    if opts.run:
        extra = [a for a in opts.pytest_extra if a != "--"]
        python = _python(root)
        with_experimental = opts.experimental or _truthy("PYRITE_PUSH_EXPERIMENTAL")
        scope = [] if with_experimental else ["-m", CORE_ONLY]
        cmd = [python, "-m", "pytest", *args, *scope, "-q", "--tb=short", *extra]
        label = (
            selection.full
            and f"full suite: {selection.full}"
            or (f"{len(selection.files)} affected test files + {len(selection.core)} core")
        )
        print(f"test-affected: {label}", file=sys.stderr)
        left_out = None if with_experimental else experimental_left_out(root, args[2:])
        if left_out and left_out[0]:
            tests, files = left_out
            print(
                f"test-affected: {tests} experimental test function{'s' * (tests != 1)} in "
                f"{files} file{'s' * (files != 1)} left out, as in CI's gating job; "
                "run them too with: scripts/test-affected --run --experimental",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
        if opts.dry_run:
            print(shlex.join(cmd))
            return 0
        needed: str | list[str] = "full" if selection.full is not None else args[2:]
        version = python_version(python)
        if version is not None and with_experimental:
            # Its own stamp key: neither scope's pass vouches for the other.
            version = f"{version}+experimental"
        # Stamps only with the worktree's own venv. The fallback interpreter
        # belongs to another checkout, whose editable installs import THAT
        # checkout's code (#210): its pass says nothing about this tree, and
        # the stamp store is shared by every worktree.
        own_venv = python == str(root / ".venv" / "bin" / "python")
        stamps = stamp_dir(root) if own_venv else None
        clean = is_clean(root)
        head_tree = tree_of(root, "HEAD")
        if (
            stamps is not None
            and version is not None
            and not (opts.force or _truthy("PYRITE_PUSH_FORCE"))
        ):
            # The tree under test: under the hook, the one being pushed --
            # never the working tree's, which may be another commit.
            looked_up = tree_of(root, to_ref) if to_ref else (clean and head_tree)
            stamp = looked_up and find_stamp(stamps, looked_up, version, needed)
            if stamp:
                print(_skip_line(looked_up, stamp), file=sys.stderr)
                return 0
        code = subprocess.call(cmd, cwd=root, env={**os.environ})
        if code == 0 and not own_venv:
            print("test-affected: passed; not stamped (no .venv in this worktree)", file=sys.stderr)
        if code == 0 and stamps is not None and version is not None:
            # pytest ran on the working tree, so that is what is stamped --
            # and only when it was exactly HEAD's tree from start to finish
            # and the whole selection ran.
            tree = tree_of(root, "HEAD")
            if not (clean and is_clean(root)) or tree is None or tree != head_tree:
                print("test-affected: passed; not stamped (uncommitted changes)", file=sys.stderr)
            elif not stampable_args(extra):
                print(
                    "test-affected: passed; not stamped "
                    "(pytest args or PYTEST_ADDOPTS/PYTEST_PLUGINS may narrow the run)",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
            else:
                write_stamp(stamps, tree, version, needed)
        return code

    for item in args[2:]:
        print(item)
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
